window.SURGERY.sections.push({
  id: "sternotomy",
  category: "foundational",
  title: "Median Sternotomy and Sternal Re-entry",
  subtitle: "The incision that opens every open-heart operation — and the hazard of doing it a second time with a beating heart or a graft stuck to the back of the bone.",
  readMins: 24,
  day: 1,
  blocks: [
    {
      title: "Start here — the mediastinal anatomy you forgot",
      figures: [{
        id: "sternotomy-retrosternal-anatomy",
        src: "assets/surgery-generated/sternotomy/retrosternal-anatomy-v1.png",
        width: 1536, height: 1024,
        alt: "Transparent sternum reveals the right ventricle, ascending aorta, brachiocephalic vein, and paired internal thoracic arteries.",
        caption: "A frontal cutaway places the right ventricle directly beneath the lower sternum. The brachiocephalic vein crosses behind the manubrium, while the internal thoracic arteries run along the inner chest wall beside the sternal edges."
      }],
      html: "<p>The sternum is a flat bone in three parts: the <b>manubrium</b> above, the <b>body</b> below, joined at the <b>manubriosternal (sternal) angle</b> — the palpable ridge at the level of the second costal cartilage — and the small cartilaginous <b>xiphoid process</b> at the tip. A median sternotomy splits this bone lengthwise, in the midline, from the sternal notch to below the xiphoid, because the sternum has no muscle belly running through its centre — it is bone over an avascular midline plane, which is exactly what makes it possible to divide it and still close it back together with wire. Superior to the manubrium, the two clavicular heads are tethered to the sternal notch by the <b>interclavicular ligament</b>, which must be divided or swept aside to fully expose the notch. Inferiorly, the <b>xiphoid</b> marks where the linea alba begins and is often excised or split to give room at the diaphragmatic end of the incision.</p><p>Now picture what sits immediately deep to that bone, because this is the anatomy that makes both a first-time and, especially, a repeat sternotomy dangerous. Directly behind the lower two-thirds of the sternum lies the <b>right ventricle</b>, separated from the bone by only the pericardium, a little fat, and in adults a variable remnant of thymic fat — there is essentially no other structure cushioning it. Behind the upper sternum lie the great vessels: the <b>ascending aorta</b> and the origin of the <b>innominate (brachiocephalic) vein</b>, which crosses transversely just deep to the manubrium. Running vertically on each side of the sternum, roughly <b>1 to 2 cm lateral to the sternal edge</b>, are the <b>internal thoracic (internal mammary) arteries</b> — the vessels you will later harvest as a coronary bypass conduit — coursing on the inner chest wall alongside their venae comitantes. Anything that has been sewn to the back of the sternum on a prior operation — the right ventricle itself, a patent bypass graft, an ascending aortic graft — is now stuck at exactly the depth the saw is about to pass through.</p><div class=\"kbox pearl\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Pearl</span> The four structures a redo sternotomy must respect, front to back as the saw or scissors advance: the sternal marrow itself (bleeds), the retrosternal adhesions (may contain RV or a graft), the right ventricle (thin free wall, directly behind the lower sternum), and, more posteriorly and to the left, the ascending aorta. CT planning exists specifically to measure how close each of these is to the bone before you cut.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "Physiology & pathophysiology — why the midline matters and why the marrow bleeds",
      html: "<p>The sternum is cut exactly in the midline for two reasons that are really the same reason: the vasculature and the biomechanics both favour it. The internal thoracic arteries run parallel to and lateral to the midline, so a cut down the centre of the bone stays maximally far from them on both sides. Mechanically, the sternum functions as a strut that the two hemithoraces hang from; a true midline cut leaves two symmetric halves of roughly equal bone stock, each still anchored to its own costal cartilages, so that closure with wires reapproximates a stable, load-sharing strut. A cut that wanders off the midline — a <b>paramedian</b> or oblique cut — leaves one side with a thin sliver of cortical bone at the wire holes. That thin fragment is mechanically weak, tends to fracture under the tension of the closure wires, and produces an <b>unstable hemisternum</b>: a segment that rocks with respiration and coughing, sets up a nidus for wire pull-through, and is a major driver of nonunion, painful clicking, and wound breakdown.</p><p>Bone marrow itself bleeds briskly and diffusely once cut, because cancellous (spongy) bone has an intrinsic blood supply from marrow sinusoids that does not vasoconstrict the way a cut artery does; this is oozing from thousands of tiny channels across the whole cut surface rather than a single bleeding point, so it cannot be controlled with a single suture or clip — it needs a physical sealant (bone wax pressed into the interstices) plus surface cautery. Left unaddressed, this marrow ooze is a real source of postoperative mediastinal blood loss and reoperation-for-bleeding risk, independent of anything happening at the heart itself.</p><p>Deep sternal wound infection is best understood as a wound-healing failure superimposed on an already precarious blood supply. The sternum's periosteal and marrow blood supply is shared with the two internal thoracic arteries; harvesting <b>both</b> ITAs for grafting (bilateral IMA harvest) removes a substantial fraction of that supply and measurably devascularizes the bone, particularly in a sternum whose microvascular healing is already impaired by <b>diabetes</b> (glycosylation-driven microangiopathy and impaired neutrophil function) or mechanically stressed by <b>obesity</b> (higher tension across the closure with fat retracting the wound edges, plus reduced local perfusion in adipose tissue). These risk factors do not act independently — they multiply.</p><div class=\"kbox danger\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Red flag</span> Bilateral internal thoracic artery harvest in a poorly controlled diabetic is the classic combination for deep sternal wound infection. If bilateral ITA grafting is planned in a diabetic or obese patient, skeletonized (rather than pedicled) harvest, which preserves more of the sternal collateral supply, and meticulous perioperative glycemic control materially lower that risk.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "Indications and pre-operative planning — first-time versus hostile re-entry",
      figures: [{
        id: "sternotomy-redo-entry-adhesions",
        src: "assets/surgery-generated/sternotomy/redo-entry-adhesions-v1.png",
        width: 1774, height: 887,
        alt: "Paired sagittal cutaways compare a clear retrosternal space with adhesions tethering heart and bypass graft close to bone.",
        caption: "Compared with a first entry, a redo sternotomy may leave little separation between posterior sternum, right ventricle, and a patent graft. Preoperative CT is used to define these relationships before re-entry."
      }],
      html: "<p>Median sternotomy is the default access for the overwhelming majority of open cardiac operations — it gives simultaneous, symmetric exposure to the right atrium, both ventricles, the aortic root and ascending aorta, and (with pericardial mobilization) reasonable access to the pulmonary artery, making it the workhorse incision for CABG, valve surgery, and proximal aortic work. Alternatives exist — a partial upper mini-sternotomy for isolated aortic valve or root work, a right anterior thoracotomy for isolated mitral or tricuspid surgery, a left thoracotomy for the descending aorta — but these trade the panoramic exposure of a full sternotomy for a smaller incision and are chosen deliberately for a specific operation, not as the default.</p><p>The planning problem is entirely different the second time. Every redo sternotomy carries a real risk of lacerating a structure adherent to the back of the bone, and the single most useful tool for lowering that risk is a <b>preoperative CT chest</b> obtained specifically to plan the re-entry. The surgeon reads it for the <b>distance from the posterior sternal table to the right ventricle, the ascending aorta, and any patent bypass graft</b> (a patent vein graft or a LIMA graft can be draped directly beneath the sternum, sometimes touching it) and for how much retrosternal fat or lung has interposed as a protective cushion since the first operation. A sternum sitting only a few millimetres off a dilated ascending aorta, a patent graft, or a thin right ventricle is a hostile re-entry, and the plan for that case changes before the skin is ever opened.</p><p>The single most important mitigation for a hostile re-entry is <b>pre-emptive peripheral cannulation</b> — establishing femoral (or axillary) arterial and venous access and, in genuinely dangerous cases, starting cardiopulmonary bypass, or at minimum having the perfusionist primed and the groin prepped and draped, <b>before</b> the sternum is reopened. If the saw or scissors lacerate the right ventricle or the aorta during re-entry, the difference between a controlled resuscitation and a fatal exsanguination is often simply whether the patient can be put on bypass immediately through vessels already exposed and cannulated.</p><div class=\"kbox pearl\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Pearl</span> \"CT before the second cut\" — for any redo sternotomy, know the sternum-to-RV, sternum-to-aorta, and sternum-to-graft distances before you scrub, and have a peripheral cannulation plan (femoral or axillary vessels exposed and ready, or already on bypass) whenever those distances are small.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "The operation, step by step — a first-time (primary) sternotomy",
      html: "<p>The patient is positioned supine with the arms tucked at the sides (to keep them out of the surgeon's and assistants' way and to allow the table to roll) and is prepped widely — chin to mid-thigh in most cases — because leg or radial-artery conduit harvest and femoral cannulation may both be needed and the field must stay sterile if the plan changes intraoperatively. A midline skin incision is made from just below the sternal notch to just below the xiphoid. Electrocautery is used to divide the subcutaneous fat down to the periosteum of the sternum, and the surgeon confirms the true bony midline by palpation and by scoring it with cautery before committing the saw, since the manubrium can appear deceptively asymmetric under fat and breast tissue.</p><p>The interclavicular ligament is divided at the top of the incision and the xiphoid is either divided or elevated at the bottom, opening a plane immediately posterior to the sternum at both ends that the surgeon's finger (or a right-angle clamp) can sweep to confirm the retrosternal space is clear of adherent structures before the saw ever passes across it — a reflex that matters even in a virgin chest, since occasionally the pericardium is unexpectedly adherent. An <b>oscillating (sagittal) saw</b> is then run down the exact midline from top to bottom in one continuous pass, splitting the manubrium and body cleanly into two symmetric halves; a reciprocating or Gigli (wire) saw is an alternative depending on surgeon preference and, particularly, in reoperative cases.</p><p>Once the sternum is split, the freshly cut marrow edges bleed diffusely and are controlled with <b>bone wax</b> pressed into the cut cortical surface plus electrocautery to the periosteal edges — this step is done before the retractor is opened, not after, because compressing the bleeding marrow surfaces early minimizes total blood loss. A sternal (Finochietto-type) retractor is then inserted and opened <b>gradually and symmetrically</b> — rushing the spread risks a sternal or costal fracture and, more urgently, can tear a retrosternal structure if the surgeon has not yet confirmed the retrosternal space is free. The pericardium is opened longitudinally in the midline, staying carefully <b>medial to both phrenic nerves</b>, which run just anterior to the pulmonary hilum on the lateral pericardial surface and, if divided, cause permanent diaphragmatic paralysis on that side. The cut pericardial edges are then suspended with stay sutures to the skin or wound retractor to make a <b>pericardial cradle</b>, which lifts and stabilizes the heart in the wound for the rest of the case.</p><div class=\"kbox danger\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Red flag</span> The phrenic nerves run on the lateral pericardial surface, not the midline — but a pericardiotomy that strays off the midline, or overly aggressive lateral dissection while creating the cradle, risks a phrenic injury. A postoperative elevated hemidiaphragm on chest X-ray after a case with an otherwise uneventful sternotomy should raise the question of phrenic injury or stretch.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "Variations — re-entry technique, alternative cannulation, and alternative access",
      html: "<p>A redo sternotomy follows the same midline principle but adds a deliberate slow-motion phase before the bone is even touched. The old wires are exposed, cut and removed first. Rather than running a saw blindly through unknown adhesions, many surgeons use a <b>reciprocating or oscillating saw with an oscillating (rather than continuously rotating) action precisely to reduce soft-tissue injury</b>, or pass a <b>Gigli (wire) saw</b> retrosternally through the old sternal cut line — using the fact that the previous midline is usually still identifiable as the path of least resistance — while a hand or instrument protects the retrosternal space from below if any space can be developed. Where imaging showed the RV, a graft, or the aorta lying immediately against the bone, the surgeon may choose to expose and cannulate the femoral vessels (or axillary artery) first and either establish bypass before completing the re-entry or simply have the field prepped and the perfusionist standing by so bypass can start within moments of a laceration.</p><p>If the retrosternal space is extremely hostile — dense adhesions with a patent graft draped across the whole posterior table, or a prior sternal infection that has obliterated any plane — some surgeons will deliberately go on peripheral bypass, decompress the heart, and only then complete the sternal division, effectively converting a blind step into a controlled one. In the rare case where the mediastinum is judged too hostile for any safe re-entry (extensive prior mediastinitis, multiple prior sternal reconstructions), an alternative access such as a lateral thoracotomy may be used instead to reach the heart from a virgin field.</p><div class=\"kbox mnemonic\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Mnemonic</span> Redo sternotomy safety sequence — <b>\"plan, expose, prime, then cut\"</b>: CT-plan the danger distances, expose peripheral vessels first if hostile, prime perfusion (cannulate or have the perfusionist ready), only then divide the bone.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "Closure — wires, weaves and plates, and what makes a sternum mechanically stable",
      figures: [{
        id: "sternotomy-closure-methods",
        src: "assets/surgery-generated/sternotomy/sternal-closure-methods-v1.png",
        width: 1536, height: 1024,
        alt: "Three anterior sternum views show transverse wires, a reinforcing longitudinal wire weave, and rigid metal plates.",
        caption: "Transverse peristernal wires, a longitudinal reinforcing weave, and rigid plates distribute closure load differently. The technique is selected according to bone quality and stability."
      }],
      html: "<p>Sternal closure is fundamentally a biomechanics problem: the two halves of bone must be compressed together firmly enough, and distributed across enough points, that the construct does not move with each breath and cough while the bone heals over the following weeks. The default technique is <b>simple peristernal wires</b> — heavy stainless-steel wire passed through or around the bone at multiple levels (commonly five to eight passes from manubrium to xiphoid) and twisted down under tension. In patients with weaker or osteoporotic bone, a wider sternal split, or any concern about a hemisternal fracture, additional patterns are added: <b>figure-of-8 wires</b> that cross the midline for extra rotational stability, or a <b>Robicsek weave</b> — a continuous longitudinal wire woven in and out along each hemisternal edge like a running whip-stitch before the transverse wires are placed — which distributes load along the whole length of each half rather than concentrating it at discrete wire holes, and is particularly used when a hemisternum is fractured, thin, or osteoporotic.</p><p>In high-risk closures — a sternum fractured during re-entry, severe osteoporosis, an obese patient, a planned bilateral ITA harvest, or simply a surgeon's preference to reduce dehiscence risk in a high-risk patient — <b>rigid plate fixation</b> (titanium plates screwed across the sternal halves, analogous to fracture plating elsewhere in the skeleton) provides substantially more mechanical rigidity than wire alone and is increasingly used primarily in these higher-risk closures. Whichever method is used, the goal is the same: eliminate micromotion at the bone edges, because micromotion is what causes wire pull-through, nonunion, and, by keeping a chronic space open at the wound, sets the stage for infection to track down to the bone.</p><div class=\"kbox pearl\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Pearl</span> Think of sternal closure on a spectrum of mechanical rigidity: simple wires, then added figure-of-8 wires, then a Robicsek weave, then rigid plates — escalate along that spectrum as bone quality worsens, the split is wider (bilateral ITA harvest, deep sternal fracture), or the patient's healing capacity is impaired (obesity, diabetes, steroids, osteoporosis).</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "Complications & how to manage them",
      html: "<p>Complications of sternotomy divide naturally into what can go wrong <b>during</b> the cut or re-entry and what can go wrong <b>afterward</b> in the closed wound. During a primary sternotomy the main intraoperative risks are marrow bleeding (managed with bone wax and cautery as described above) and, rarely, an unrecognized pleural or pericardial adhesion; during a <b>redo</b> sternotomy the feared intraoperative complication is direct laceration of the right ventricle, a patent graft, or the aorta by the saw or scissors, which is why the CT-based danger-distance assessment and pre-emptive peripheral cannulation described earlier exist — if it happens, immediate digital or sponge-stick pressure on the injury while the perfusionist establishes bypass through already-prepared femoral vessels is the standard rescue.</p><p>In the healed or healing wound, <b>sternal dehiscence</b> is mechanical failure of the closure — wires pulling through bone, cutting loose, or a hemisternal fracture propagating — presenting as a clicking or rocking sternum, increasing pain with movement or coughing, and sometimes a visibly widening or draining wound; it is confirmed clinically (a positive \"click\" on gentle sternal compression) or on CT, and stable but symptomatic dehiscence without infection is typically managed with sternal re-wiring or plating, while dehiscence with infection is managed as below.</p><p><b>Deep sternal wound infection (mediastinitis)</b> is the complication that most changes a patient's course. Risk factors are additive and include diabetes/poor glycemic control, obesity, bilateral ITA harvest (especially pedicled, in a diabetic), prolonged operative or bypass time, reoperation, and postoperative low cardiac output requiring prolonged ventilation or chest compressions. The organisms are predominantly skin flora — <b>Staphylococcus aureus and coagulase-negative staphylococci (S. epidermidis)</b> most commonly, with gram-negative organisms seen in a meaningful minority, particularly in delayed presentations. Presentation ranges from localized wound erythema and drainage to overt sternal instability with purulent discharge, fever, and leukocytosis, sometimes with bacteremia. Management is surgical, not just antibiotic: <b>aggressive debridement</b> of all infected and necrotic bone and soft tissue, culture-directed intravenous antibiotics, and either a staged approach using <b>negative-pressure wound therapy (NPWT)</b> to control the wound and promote granulation before definitive closure, or single-stage debridement with immediate <b>muscle flap reconstruction</b> — most often bilateral <b>pectoralis major advancement flaps</b>, with an <b>omental flap</b> (based on the gastroepiploic vessels, tunnelled up from the abdomen) reserved for larger defects or when pectoralis coverage is insufficient, because omentum is highly vascular and has intrinsic angiogenic and immunologic properties that help clear residual infection.</p><div class=\"kbox danger\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Red flag</span> A patient a week or two post-sternotomy with new sternal instability, a widening or draining wound, fever, or unexplained leukocytosis has mediastinitis until proven otherwise. This is a surgical emergency requiring prompt debridement — delaying source control while cycling through oral antibiotics risks progression to overt mediastinitis and sepsis.</div>"
    },
    {
      title: "Pimping ceiling — classifications, evidence and the fine print",
      html: "<p>Deep sternal wound infection is formally staged for both severity and timing. The <b>El Oakley and Wright classification of mediastinitis</b> grades by time of onset and risk-factor burden (types I through IV, factoring in early versus late presentation and the presence of risk factors and prior failed treatment), which helps standardize how aggressively to treat and how to counsel prognosis. Superficial and deep sternal wound infections are also commonly described by the older <b>Pairolero classification</b> of sternal wound infection into three types by timing and depth of presentation (acute serosanguinous drainage without osteomyelitis; a more delayed presentation with chondritis/osteomyelitis; and a chronic, indolent draining sinus presenting months to years later, sometimes associated with a low-grade wire-related infection). Know that both classification systems exist to communicate severity, guide the choice between staged NPWT-then-flap versus single-stage debridement-and-flap, and predict which patients need the most aggressive source control.</p><p>On the evidence for prevention: the <b>ART (Arterial Revascularization Trial)</b>, designed primarily to compare single versus bilateral internal thoracic artery grafting for long-term survival and graft patency, found that bilateral ITA harvest carries a materially higher risk of deep sternal wound infection than single ITA harvest, an effect that is most pronounced in <b>diabetic and obese</b> patients — this is why skeletonized (rather than pedicled) bilateral harvest, which preserves more of the sternal collateral blood supply, and strict perioperative glycemic control are emphasized whenever bilateral ITA grafting is planned. Perioperative glycemic control protocols targeting blood glucose in a defined range (avoiding both hyperglycemia and iatrogenic hypoglycemia) in the immediate postoperative period have been repeatedly associated with lower deep sternal wound infection rates and are now standard of care in most cardiac surgery programs. Negative-pressure wound therapy, applied either as definitive staged treatment or as a bridge to flap closure, has become the modern standard for managing an open infected sternotomy wound because it controls exudate, reduces bacterial burden, and promotes granulation tissue formation faster than conventional dressing changes, shortening the interval to definitive closure.</p><div class=\"kbox mnemonic\"><span class=\"kbox-tag\">Mnemonic</span> DSWI risk factors to recite under pressure — <b>\"DOBBY\": Diabetes, Obesity, Bilateral ITA harvest, Bypass (prolonged CPB/operative time), reoperativitY (redo sternotomy)</b> — the more of these that stack in one patient, the harder you fight for skeletonized harvest, tight glycemic control, and a low threshold to debride early if the wound looks wrong.</div>"
    }
  ],
  steps: [
    "Position the patient supine with arms tucked and prep widely (chin to knees) in case conduit harvest or femoral access is needed",
    "For a redo case, confirm the CT-based re-entry plan and establish or prepare pre-emptive femoral or axillary access before opening the skin",
    "Make a midline skin incision from the sternal notch to below the xiphoid",
    "Divide subcutaneous tissue with cautery down to the sternal periosteum and confirm the true bony midline before sawing",
    "Divide the interclavicular ligament superiorly and free or split the xiphoid inferiorly to open the retrosternal plane at both ends",
    "Sweep a finger or right-angle clamp behind the sternum at both ends to confirm the retrosternal space is clear before the saw passes",
    "Divide the sternum in the exact midline with an oscillating or reciprocating saw (or a Gigli/wire saw and slow adhesiolysis in a hostile redo)",
    "Achieve marrow hemostasis on both cut edges with bone wax and electrocautery before spreading the retractor",
    "Insert the sternal retractor and open it gradually and symmetrically",
    "Open the pericardium longitudinally in the midline, staying medial to both phrenic nerves",
    "Suspend the cut pericardial edges as a pericardial cradle to stabilize the heart in the wound",
    "Proceed with the planned cardiac procedure",
    "Achieve hemostasis, place mediastinal (and pleural, if opened) drains, and loosely reapproximate the pericardium",
    "Close the sternum with peristernal wires (adding figure-of-8 wires, a Robicsek weave, or rigid plate fixation for a wider split, fractured hemisternum, or high-risk bone)",
    "Close the presternal fascia, subcutaneous tissue and skin in layers",
    "Monitor postoperatively for sternal stability (a negative click on gentle compression) and for early signs of wound infection or dehiscence"
  ]
});

